Take a look at https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/57871f64bdea450c1f04c9a53dc1a78223719164/src/oracle/BalancerOracle.sol#L67-L89

```solidity
constructor(
  address balancerVault_,
  address chainlinkOracle_,
  address pool_,
  uint256 updateWaitWindow_,
  uint256 stalePeriod_
) initializer {
  balancerVault = IVault(balancerVault_);
  updateWaitWindow = updateWaitWindow_;
  stalePeriod = stalePeriod_;
  chainlinkOracle = IOracle(chainlinkOracle_);
  pool = pool_;
  poolId = IWeightedPool(pool).getPoolId();

  (address[] memory tokens, , ) = balancerVault.getPoolTokens(poolId);

  // store the tokens
  uint256 len = tokens.length; //@audit what if one of the tokens gets deregistered?
  token0 = (len > 0) ? tokens[0] : address(0);
  token1 = (len > 1) ? tokens[1] : address(0);
  token2 = (len > 2) ? tokens[2] : address(0);
}

```

Evidently, the _[immutable tokens](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/57871f64bdea450c1f04c9a53dc1a78223719164/src/oracle/BalancerOracle.sol#L38-L42)_ are being initialized in the constructor's execution, issue however is that some Balancer pools have [a _deregisterToken()_ function](https://github.com/balancer/balancer-v2-monorepo/blob/227683919a7031615c0bc7f144666cdf3883d212/pkg/vault/contracts/PoolTokens.sol#L59) where a _token_ gets deleted from the “pool” so in our case here, if that happens when getting the price [we'd end up pricing the wrong tokens](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/57871f64bdea450c1f04c9a53dc1a78223719164/src/oracle/BalancerOracle.sol#L166-L174) and as such have the oracle [update a wrong price as the safe/current one](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/57871f64bdea450c1f04c9a53dc1a78223719164/src/oracle/BalancerOracle.sol#L114-L137).

```solidity

